{"type":"text","timestamp":1792512933656,"sessionID":"ses_ea5ec442cffeCxRon1oRLSyS4A","part":{"id":"prt_15a18a9b6001Utmplxr6LZrai7","messageID":"msg_15a17fe6c0014qtE0fv9Hf631c","sessionID":"ses_ea5ec442cffeCxRon1oRLSyS4A","type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I understand the measurement: `work_ratio` = candidate wall-clock / offline (numpy partition-refinement) wall-clock, summed over init + every update. Both do a full recompute, so the ratio is a constant-factor comparison. My plan: a **bitmask partition-refinement LexBFS** in pure Python (bit ops are far faster than numpy scalar accesses + set lookups), which should land well below 1.0.\n\nLet me write the first candidate.\n\n","time":{"start":1792512928182,"end":1792512933650}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792512933689,"sessionID":"ses_ea5ec442cffeCxRon1oRLSyS4A","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8424179a9d3ddaf8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s3/candidate.py","content":"\"\"\"Dynamic LexBFS ordering (undirected).\n\nMaintains a valid lexicographic BFS ordering under edge insertions and\ndeletions. The ordering is recomputed with a bitmask partition-refinement\nLexBFS (Habib-McConnell-Paul-Viennot), which is correct for any start vertex\nand any tie-break. Bitmask partitioning is much cheaper than the numpy\noffline baseline's per-vertex scalar accesses and set membership tests, so\nthe per-update work lands well below a full numpy recompute.\n\nState: {\"adj\": list[int] (adjacency bitmask per vertex), \"n\": int,\n        \"ordering\": list[int] (a valid LexBFS ordering)}.\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef _lexbfs(adj, n):\n    \"\"\"Return a valid LexBFS ordering of the graph with adjacency bitmasks adj.\n\n    Partition refinement: cells partition the unplaced vertices; two vertices\n    share a cell iff they have identical adjacency to every placed vertex.\n    Each step places the lowest vertex of the first cell and refines every\n    cell by that vertex's adjacency (adjacent part before non-adjacent part).\n    \"\"\"\n    order = []\n    full = (1 << n) - 1\n    cells = [full]\n    for _ in range(n):\n        c0 = cells[0]\n        pbit = c0 & -c0\n        p = pbit.bit_length() - 1\n        cells[0] = c0 & ~pbit\n        order.append(p)\n        ap = adj[p]\n        nap = full ^ ap\n        newcells = []\n        for c in cells:\n            if not c:\n                continue\n            a = c & ap\n            b = c & nap\n            if a:\n                newcells.append(a)\n            if b:\n                newcells.append(b)\n        cells = newcells\n    return order\n\n\ndef init(graph):\n    n = len(graph)\n    adj = [0] * n\n    for u in range(n):\n        row = graph[u]\n        m = 0\n        for v in range(n):\n            if row[v]:\n                m |= 1 << v\n        adj[u] = m\n    return {\"adj\": adj, \"n\": n, \"ordering\": _lexbfs(adj, n)}\n\n\ndef insert_edge(graph, edge, state):\n    u, v = edge\n    adj = state[\"adj\"]\n    adj[u] |= 1 << v\n    adj[v] |= 1 << u\n    return {\"adj\": adj, \"n\": state[\"n\"], \"ordering\": _lexbfs(adj, state[\"n\"])}\n\n\ndef delete_edge(graph, edge, state):\n    u, v = edge\n    adj = state[\"adj\"]\n    adj[u] &= ~(1 << v)\n    adj[v] &= ~(1 << u)\n    return {\"adj\": adj, \"n\": state[\"n\"], \"ordering\": _lexbfs(adj, state[\"n\"])}\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s3/candidate.py","exists":true,"truncated":false},"title":"runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s3/candidate.py","time":{"start":1792512933641,"end":1792512933683}},"id":"prt_15a18accd001GhFQ5VyJk79bWF","sessionID":"ses_ea5ec442cffeCxRon1oRLSyS4A","messageID":"msg_15a17fe6c0014qtE0fv9Hf631c"}}
