{"type":"text","timestamp":1792513125546,"sessionID":"ses_ea5ec442cffeCxRon1oRLSyS4A","part":{"id":"prt_15a1b97790013nRCgx42GOhNrZ","messageID":"msg_15a1aa275001m6ZcW9vzf4tYTs","sessionID":"ses_ea5ec442cffeCxRon1oRLSyS4A","type":"text","text":"\n\nCheck 1 passed at `work_ratio = 0.155` (6.4× below the numpy baseline). Full correctness achieved.\n\nNow let me analyze whether I can push it lower. Key insight: the verifier's (LB) check is O(n³), while my partition-refinement recompute is O(n²) — so for n≤5, even *verifying* a candidate ordering is more expensive than recomputing it. This means recompute is near the practical floor, and a true dynamic algorithm would need o(n²) work (the open problem). Still, let me try to shave the constant factor with a tighter inner loop (one fewer AND per cell via `b = c ^ a`).\n\n","time":{"start":1792513120121,"end":1792513125541}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792513125601,"sessionID":"ses_ea5ec442cffeCxRon1oRLSyS4A","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-903c24f032dc1b36","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s3/candidate.py","content":"\"\"\"Dynamic LexBFS ordering (undirected).\n\nMaintains a valid lexicographic BFS ordering under edge insertions and\ndeletions. The ordering is recomputed with a bitmask partition-refinement\nLexBFS (Habib-McConnell-Paul-Viennot), which is correct for any start vertex\nand any tie-break. Bitmask partitioning is much cheaper than the numpy\noffline baseline's per-vertex scalar accesses and set membership tests, so\nthe per-update work lands well below a full numpy recompute.\n\nState: {\"adj\": list[int] (adjacency bitmask per vertex), \"n\": int,\n        \"ordering\": list[int] (a valid LexBFS ordering)}.\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef _lexbfs(adj, n):\n    \"\"\"Return a valid LexBFS ordering of the graph with adjacency bitmasks adj.\n\n    Partition refinement: cells partition the unplaced vertices; two vertices\n    share a cell iff they have identical adjacency to every placed vertex.\n    Each step places the lowest vertex of the first cell and refines every\n    cell by that vertex's adjacency (adjacent part before non-adjacent part).\n    \"\"\"\n    order = []\n    oapp = order.append\n    full = (1 << n) - 1\n    cells = [full]\n    for _ in range(n):\n        c0 = cells[0]\n        pbit = c0 & -c0\n        p = pbit.bit_length() - 1\n        cells[0] = c0 ^ pbit\n        oapp(p)\n        ap = adj[p]\n        nc = []\n        app = nc.append\n        for c in cells:\n            if c:\n                a = c & ap\n                if a:\n                    app(a)\n                b = c ^ a  # c & (full ^ ap); a = c & ap so XOR strips it\n                if b:\n                    app(b)\n        cells = nc\n    return order\n\n\ndef init(graph):\n    n = len(graph)\n    adj = [0] * n\n    for u in range(n):\n        row = graph[u]\n        m = 0\n        for v in range(n):\n            if row[v]:\n                m |= 1 << v\n        adj[u] = m\n    return {\"adj\": adj, \"n\": n, \"ordering\": _lexbfs(adj, n)}\n\n\ndef insert_edge(graph, edge, state):\n    u, v = edge\n    adj = state[\"adj\"]\n    adj[u] |= 1 << v\n    adj[v] |= 1 << u\n    return {\"adj\": adj, \"n\": state[\"n\"], \"ordering\": _lexbfs(adj, state[\"n\"])}\n\n\ndef delete_edge(graph, edge, state):\n    u, v = edge\n    adj = state[\"adj\"]\n    adj[u] &= ~(1 << v)\n    adj[v] &= ~(1 << u)\n    return {\"adj\": adj, \"n\": state[\"n\"], \"ordering\": _lexbfs(adj, state[\"n\"])}\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s3/candidate.py","exists":true,"truncated":false},"title":"runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s3/candidate.py","time":{"start":1792513125532,"end":1792513125594}},"id":"prt_15a1b9bef001SXqOeezAppii9j","sessionID":"ses_ea5ec442cffeCxRon1oRLSyS4A","messageID":"msg_15a1aa275001m6ZcW9vzf4tYTs"}}
